.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt FSCK_EXFAT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fsck_exfat
.Nd exFAT file system consistency checker and repair utility
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl p
.Op Fl BCFfvy
.Ar filesystem ...
.Nm
.Op Fl BCFfnyv
.Ar filesystem ...
.Nm
.Fl l
.Ar filesystem ...
.Nm
.Fl L Ar label
.Ar filesystem ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility verifies and repairs exFAT
file systems.
.Pp
The first form of
.Nm
preens the specified file systems.
It is normally started by
.Xr fsck 8
from
.Pa /etc/rc
during automatic reboot when an exFAT file system is detected.
When preening file systems,
.Nm
will fix minor, non-destructive inconsistencies automatically.
If potentially destructive repairs are required (such as removing
corrupted directory entry sets or truncating cross-linked chains),
.Nm
will not modify those files and will exit with an error code indicating
that manual intervention is required.
.Pp
The second form of
.Nm
checks the specified file systems interactively, requesting confirmation
before making any modifications, unless the
.Fl y
or
.Fl n
options are specified.
.Pp
The consistency check is performed in four distinct phases:
.Bl -tag -width "Phase 4"
.It Sy "Phase 1 - Verifying Boot Sector and Volume Boot Record"
Verifies the Main and Backup Volume Boot Records (VBRs), boot signatures,
sector and cluster sizing, cluster count bounds, FAT and heap layout,
volume geometry, and 32-bit boot region checksums
across sectors 0 through 11 and 12 through 23.
If one boot region is intact while the other is corrupted,
.Nm
offers to restore the damaged region from the valid copy.
For single-FAT volumes
.Pq Li NumberOfFats == 1 ,
the
.Va ActiveFat
flag in
.Va VolumeFlags
is validated and cleared if improperly set per section 3.1.13.1.
The
.Va ClearToZero
flag
.Pq section 3.1.13.4
and reserved bits in
.Va VolumeFlags
.Pq section 3.1.13.5
are validated and cleared if set.
For TexFAT volumes containing two file allocation tables
.Pq NumberOfFats == 2 ,
the active and secondary FAT tables are compared sector-by-sector and can
be synchronized.
Volume cleanliness
.Pq EXFAT_VOL_DIRTY
is evaluated; if the volume is clean and preening mode
.Pq Fl p
is active, further checks are skipped.
.It Sy "Phase 2 - Scanning Volume Metadata"
Locates and validates the root directory system entries: the Allocation
Bitmap (entry type 0x81), the Up-Case Table (0x82), and the Volume Label
(0x83), ensuring that the Volume Label contains valid characters per the
specification and does not exceed 11 characters.
Also validates recognized benign primary entries including Volume GUID (0xA0)
and TexFAT Padding (0xA1) entries, ensuring that Volume GUID and TexFAT Padding
entries have a secondary count of zero and primary flags of zero.
TexFAT Padding entries have their reserved field zeroed if non-zero per
section 7.10 of the exFAT specification.
Volume GUID entries containing an all-zero GUID (null GUID) are detected
and offered for removal or zeroing.
If the Allocation Bitmap directory entry is missing,
.Nm
can recreate the entry and link it to the allocation bitmap clusters.
If the Up-Case Table directory entry is missing or damaged,
.Nm
can generate and write a standard 128 KiB UTF-16 case folding table,
compute its 32-bit boot checksum, and recreate the directory entry.
The Up-Case table is decompressed, its checksum is verified, and the
volume allocation bitmap is loaded into memory.
.It Sy "Phase 3 - Checking Directory Tree and File Streams"
Recursively traverses all directories starting from the root directory.
Validates directory entry sets, entry set checksums
.Pq Li fe_set_checksum ,
secondary Stream Extension entries (0xC0), File Name entries (0xC1),
and 16-bit up-cased file name hashes
.Pq Li se_name_hash .
.Nm
performs the following consistency repairs:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Orphaned secondary directory entries (entries with bit 5 set encountered
without an active primary entry) are removed.
.It
Corrupted directory entry sets with invalid secondary counts or missing
Stream Extension entries are removed.
.It
Directory entry set checksum mismatches are corrected by recomputing
the 16-bit checksum over the entry set.
.It
Incorrect file name hashes are recalculated and updated in the Stream
Extension entry.
.It
General secondary flags
.Pq Li GeneralSecondaryFlags
are validated and corrected per exFAT specification section 6.4.2:
.Va AllocationPossible
must be set for Stream Extension and Vendor Allocation entries and cleared for
File Name and Vendor Extension entries;
.Va NoFatChain
is verified to be set only when
.Va AllocationPossible
is set; and reserved bits (bits 2 through 7) are cleared.
.It
File directory entry timestamps and attributes are validated and sanitized per
exFAT specification section 7.4:
reserved attribute bits in
.Va FileAttributes
are cleared;
10ms increment values in
.Va Create10msIncrement
and
.Va LastModified10msIncrement
exceeding 199 (1.99 seconds) are clamped to zero per section 7.4.9;
UTC offset values in
.Va CreateUtcOffset ,
.Va LastModifiedUtcOffset ,
and
.Va LastAccessedUtcOffset
outside the valid range of -128 to +127 per section 7.4.10 are normalized;
and reserved fields
.Pq Li Reserved1 No and Li Reserved2
are zeroed per sections 1.2 and 7.4.
.It
File names are validated per exFAT Specification section 7.7.3:
file names containing invalid characters (code points below 0x0020,
quotes, asterisks, slashes, colons, angle brackets, question marks,
backslashes, or pipes) or names consisting entirely of spaces are reported
as file system errors.
.It
Stream
.Li ValidDataLength
is clamped to
.Li DataLength
for files, and synchronized with
.Li DataLength
for directory streams.
.It
Superfluous clusters are dropped: zero-length files that allocate clusters
have their chains freed and allocation flags cleared; files with FAT
chains exceeding
.Li DataLength
are truncated with an
.Li EXFAT_FAT_EOF
marker at the expected length and trailing clusters are freed.
.It
Cross-linked cluster chains are detected: subsequent claimants referencing
a cluster already claimed by another file are truncated before the
collision point.
.It
Cluster chains referencing defective clusters
.Pq Li EXFAT_FAT_BAD
are truncated at the preceding valid cluster.
.It
Cycles in cluster chains are detected and guarded against.
.It
Root-only primary directory entries (such as Volume GUID, Volume Label,
Allocation Bitmap, and Up-Case Table) encountered outside the root directory
are detected and removed.
.It
Unrecognized critical directory entries
.Pq entry type codes 0x80 through 0x9F
are detected and offered for removal per exFAT specification section 6.2.1.
.El
.It Sy "Phase 4 - Checking Cluster Allocation and Consistency"
Reconciles the set of referenced clusters tracked during Phase 3 against
the on-disk Allocation Bitmap.
Orphaned clusters (marked allocated on disk but unreferenced by any valid
file or directory) can be recovered into the
.Pa LOST.DIR
directory in the root directory.
If
.Pa LOST.DIR
does not exist,
.Nm
offers to create it as a standard directory, creating files named after each
chain's starting cluster number (e.g.,
.Pa LOST.DIR/12345 ) .
If a file with that name already exists in
.Pa LOST.DIR ,
a unique numeric suffix is appended (e.g.,
.Pa LOST.DIR/12345_1 )
to avoid collisions while remaining within single-entry name length bounds.
Alternatively, orphaned clusters can be cleared in the allocation bitmap.
Clusters that are in use but marked free on disk are marked allocated.
Finally, the updated allocation bitmap is committed to disk.
.El
.Pp
Upon clean completion of all checking phases,
.Nm
clears the dirty flag
.Pq EXFAT_VOL_DIRTY
in both Main and Backup Boot Sectors and recalculates the on-disk
.Li PercentInUse
allocation percentage.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl B
Ignored for
.Xr fsck 8
compatibility.
.It Fl C
Ignored for
.Xr fsck 8
compatibility.
.It Fl F
Compatibility check for background file system checking.
Because exFAT file systems must always be checked in the foreground,
this option exits immediately with exit status 5.
.It Fl f
Force checking of the file system even if it was marked cleanly unmounted.
.It Fl l
Display the volume label of the specified file system and exit.
.It Fl L Ar label
Set the volume label of the specified file system to
.Ar label
and exit.
If an empty label
.Pq Qq ""
is specified, the volume label is deleted.
The label may be up to 11 characters in length and must not contain
control characters (0x0000 through 0x001F) or any of:
.Ql \&" ,
.Ql * ,
.Ql / ,
.Ql : ,
.Ql < ,
.Ql > ,
.Ql \&? ,
.Ql \e ,
or
.Ql | .
.It Fl n
Assume a
.Dq no
response to all questions; do not open the file system for writing.
.It Fl p
Preen mode.
Perform automatic non-interactive checking and apply minor,
non-destructive repairs automatically.
In this mode,
.Nm
will restore boot sector checksums, clear invalid ActiveFat flags on
single-FAT volumes, clear the volume dirty flag,
update the allocation percentage, remove orphaned secondary directory entries,
recompute directory entry set checksums, repair file name hashes,
normalize secondary entry flags, zero reserved fields and attributes,
sanitize timestamp 10ms increments and UTC offsets,
clamp valid data lengths, drop superfluous file clusters, reconnect orphaned
clusters to
.Pa LOST.DIR
with collision avoidance, and reconcile the allocation bitmap.
Potentially destructive actions (such as deleting corrupted directory
entry sets, truncating cross-linked chains, or truncating at bad clusters)
are not performed in preen mode unless
.Fl y
is also given.
.It Fl v
Verbose mode.
Displays detailed information including volume serial number, file system
revision, sector and cluster sizing, bad cluster count, and volume label.
.It Fl y
Assume a
.Dq yes
response to all questions, repairing detected inconsistencies.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits with one of the following values:
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It 0
The file system is clean and no errors were found.
.It 1
Inconsistencies were found and corrected.
.It 2
Inconsistencies were corrected; a system reboot is recommended.
.It 4
Inconsistencies were found but not corrected.
.It 5
Background checking is not supported; foreground check required (returned by
.Fl F ) .
.It 8
An operational error occurred (such as inability to open the device).
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr exfat 4 ,
.Xr fsck 8 ,
.Xr glabel 8 ,
.Xr mount_exfat 8 ,
.Xr newfs_exfat 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility first appeared in
.Fx 15.1 .
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm
utility was developed for the
.Fx
Project.
